1
00:00:00,167 --> 00:00:01,922
So why is Texas Republican?

2
00:00:02,225 --> 00:00:03,989
On Tuesday, it was not.

3
00:00:04,459 --> 00:00:09,030
On November the 3rd, 2026, Texas
elected a Democratic senator for the

4
00:00:09,033 --> 00:00:14,836
first time since 1988, and a Democratic
governor for the first time since 1990.

5
00:00:15,049 --> 00:00:18,291
You have probably heard the easy
explanation already, but here

6
00:00:18,291 --> 00:00:20,027
is what almost nobody is saying.

7
00:00:20,384 --> 00:00:21,918
Texas has done this before.

8
00:00:22,208 --> 00:00:25,987
For about 100 years, Texas was
a one-party Democratic state.

9
00:00:26,258 --> 00:00:30,613
Then for 30 years, a one-party
Republican state, and both

10
00:00:30,613 --> 00:00:32,464
parties lost it the same way.

11
00:00:32,976 --> 00:00:36,586
They got so comfortable they
forgot they could lose, and then

12
00:00:36,596 --> 00:00:38,175
their own voters walked away.

13
00:00:38,206 --> 00:00:41,318
I went through 190 years
of Texas election results.

14
00:00:41,429 --> 00:00:44,610
This is the story of people
who flipped Texas, and most

15
00:00:44,611 --> 00:00:46,289
of them aren't who you think.

16
00:00:49,693 --> 00:00:52,552
To understand Tuesday, you
have to understand one thing.

17
00:00:52,916 --> 00:00:57,388
Texas has changed hands more than
almost any place in America, and

18
00:00:57,495 --> 00:01:01,187
every handover left something behind
that still shapes how Texans vote.

19
00:01:02,205 --> 00:01:04,116
Six different flags have flown over it.

20
00:01:04,691 --> 00:01:09,039
Long before any of them, this land
belonged to dozens of Native nations.

21
00:01:09,387 --> 00:01:11,136
The Caddo farmed the east.

22
00:01:11,476 --> 00:01:13,423
The Karankawa faced the coast.

23
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:16,502
And on the plains, Comanche
built one of the most powerful

24
00:01:16,515 --> 00:01:18,497
horse empires in North America

25
00:01:21,561 --> 00:01:23,009
Flag number one, Spain.

26
00:01:23,288 --> 00:01:27,210
In 1718, Spanish missionaries found a
mission beside the San Antonio River.

27
00:01:27,483 --> 00:01:29,316
You know it by a
different name, The Alamo.

28
00:01:29,884 --> 00:01:31,372
Flag number two, France.

29
00:01:31,675 --> 00:01:35,750
For a few years in 1680s, a French
explorer named La Salle planted

30
00:01:35,750 --> 00:01:38,068
a little fort on the Texas coast.

31
00:01:38,495 --> 00:01:39,304
It did not go well.

32
00:01:39,531 --> 00:01:42,143
Disease, hunger, and attacks wiped it out.

33
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:47,352
Spain held on 100 years, but it
never really filled the place.

34
00:01:47,548 --> 00:01:53,059
By the early 1800s, Spanish Texas
had only a few thousand settlers.

35
00:01:53,555 --> 00:01:54,777
Hold on to that empty border.

36
00:01:54,918 --> 00:02:00,533
In Texas, whoever fills the land ends up
running it, and that is still true in 2026

37
00:02:03,484 --> 00:02:05,197
Flag number three, Mexico.

38
00:02:05,431 --> 00:02:11,118
In 1821, Mexico won its independence from
Spain and inherited a giant empty Texas.

39
00:02:11,539 --> 00:02:13,516
So Mexico tried something risky.

40
00:02:14,014 --> 00:02:15,817
It invited Americans in.

41
00:02:16,272 --> 00:02:20,396
A man named Stephen F. Austin
bought the first 300 families.

42
00:02:20,742 --> 00:02:21,448
Land was cheap.

43
00:02:21,882 --> 00:02:25,377
Tens of thousand followed, and one
of them bought something Mexico

44
00:02:25,377 --> 00:02:27,515
was moving to ban, enslaved people.

45
00:02:27,915 --> 00:02:30,676
Mexico abolished slavery in 1829.

46
00:02:30,849 --> 00:02:33,927
Texas settlers looked for
loopholes, labeling enslaved

47
00:02:33,931 --> 00:02:35,916
workers as indentured servants.

48
00:02:36,370 --> 00:02:41,476
By the 1830s, Americans in Texas
outnumbered Mexican by about 10 to one.

49
00:02:41,787 --> 00:02:43,617
Mexico tightened its grip.

50
00:02:43,766 --> 00:02:45,076
Texans pushed back.

51
00:02:45,194 --> 00:02:48,363
It was the first time newcomers
changed who ran Texas.

52
00:02:48,778 --> 00:02:50,064
It would not be the last

53
00:02:52,410 --> 00:02:56,567
In 1835, the Mexican army came
to take back a small cannon

54
00:02:56,569 --> 00:02:58,099
from the town of Gonzales.

55
00:02:58,484 --> 00:03:01,541
The settlers made a flag with
a picture of the cannons and

56
00:03:01,541 --> 00:03:03,956
two words, "Come and take it."

57
00:03:04,379 --> 00:03:06,472
That was the start of Texas Revolution.

58
00:03:06,968 --> 00:03:12,809
Mexico's president, General Antonio
Lopez de Santa Anna, marched north

59
00:03:12,809 --> 00:03:14,590
with thousands of soldiers to crush it.

60
00:03:15,284 --> 00:03:18,403
In February 1836, he reached San Antonio.

61
00:03:18,893 --> 00:03:23,909
Inside the old mission, the Alamo, were
found 200 Texans defender, including a

62
00:03:23,911 --> 00:03:26,565
frontier celebrity named Davy Crockett.

63
00:03:26,929 --> 00:03:28,610
They held out for 13 days.

64
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:33,396
On the morning of March the
6th, 1836, the Alamo fell.

65
00:03:34,019 --> 00:03:35,957
Nearly every defender was killed.

66
00:03:36,471 --> 00:03:38,590
Hundreds of Mexican soldiers died, too.

67
00:03:39,195 --> 00:03:41,637
But Santa Anna had made a mistake.

68
00:03:41,905 --> 00:03:46,652
He had given the Texans a battle
cry, and then he made a second one.

69
00:03:47,069 --> 00:03:50,132
That is, he split up his
army to chase them and pushed

70
00:03:50,142 --> 00:03:51,743
ahead with only part of it.

71
00:03:52,064 --> 00:03:56,529
Four days before the Alamo fell,
Texans delegates had already declared

72
00:03:56,545 --> 00:04:03,543
independence, and on April the 21st, 1836,
at San Jacinto, Sam Houston's army caught

73
00:04:03,547 --> 00:04:05,930
Santa Anna camp during the afternoon rest.

74
00:04:06,055 --> 00:04:07,007
Remember the Alamo?

75
00:04:07,488 --> 00:04:09,631
The battle lasted about 18 minutes.

76
00:04:10,268 --> 00:04:13,504
Santa Anna was captured the
next day hiding in the grass.

77
00:04:13,529 --> 00:04:15,768
To get his freedom, he signed away Texas

78
00:04:18,765 --> 00:04:19,399
Flag number four.

79
00:04:19,594 --> 00:04:23,382
For nearly 10 years, Texas was its
own country, the Republic of Texas.

80
00:04:23,421 --> 00:04:28,714
It had its own president, its own
army, its own money, and not much else.

81
00:04:28,801 --> 00:04:29,566
It was broke.

82
00:04:29,652 --> 00:04:34,279
It had a war with the Comanche, a hostile
Mexico, and debts it could not pay,

83
00:04:34,378 --> 00:04:36,226
and its constitution protected slavery.

84
00:04:36,258 --> 00:04:40,036
It even made it hard for feedback
people to live there at all.

85
00:04:40,092 --> 00:04:45,217
So most Texans wanted to join the United
States, but up north, that was a problem.

86
00:04:45,414 --> 00:04:48,241
Adding Texas meant adding
a giant new slave state.

87
00:04:48,669 --> 00:04:52,561
In Washington, it turned
out into a nine-year brawl.

88
00:04:52,804 --> 00:04:58,080
Finally, on December the 20th,
1845, Texas became the 28th state.

89
00:04:58,443 --> 00:05:01,775
Flag number six is coming,
but first, flag number five.

90
00:05:02,155 --> 00:05:04,042
Annexation also started a war.

91
00:05:04,395 --> 00:05:07,039
Texas said its border was the Rio Grande.

92
00:05:07,051 --> 00:05:10,244
It was the Neuse River,
more than 100 miles north.

93
00:05:10,616 --> 00:05:12,780
The United States sent
troops into the gap.

94
00:05:12,977 --> 00:05:17,751
The Mexican-American War followed,
and by 1848, Mexico had lost not

95
00:05:17,751 --> 00:05:22,058
just Texas, but California and
most of the Americans' Southwest.

96
00:05:22,623 --> 00:05:27,745
Now, here is where our story really
starts because in 1840s Texas, the

97
00:05:27,756 --> 00:05:29,614
dominant party was the Democrats.

98
00:05:29,948 --> 00:05:32,775
The national Democratic Party
of the time was the party of the

99
00:05:32,775 --> 00:05:37,251
South, the party of expansion, and
the party that defended slavery.

100
00:05:37,616 --> 00:05:41,546
Remember that, because the parties
we know today were about to be

101
00:05:41,546 --> 00:05:45,770
born, and Texas was about to choose
a side that would last a century

102
00:05:50,714 --> 00:05:54,872
In the 1850s, a new party was born
in the North, the Republican Party.

103
00:05:55,179 --> 00:05:59,198
Its founding cause was stopping slavery
from spreading into new territory.

104
00:05:59,637 --> 00:06:03,395
In 1860, its candidate, Abraham
Lincoln, won the presidency.

105
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:06,130
In Texas, he was not even on the ballot.

106
00:06:06,471 --> 00:06:10,996
By then, there were about 180,000
enslaved people in Texas, and almost

107
00:06:10,997 --> 00:06:13,073
a third of everyone in the state.

108
00:06:13,611 --> 00:06:15,144
Texas called a convention.

109
00:06:15,308 --> 00:06:19,770
In February 1861, voters approved
secession, about three to one.

110
00:06:20,014 --> 00:06:22,411
The convention's own
declaration said, why?

111
00:06:22,759 --> 00:06:23,857
To protect slavery.

112
00:06:24,597 --> 00:06:30,496
One famous Texan said, "No." Governor Sam
Houston, the hero of San Jacinto, refused

113
00:06:30,507 --> 00:06:34,334
to show loyalty to the Confederacy,
so they removed him from the office.

114
00:06:34,579 --> 00:06:36,676
Flag number five, the Confederacy.

115
00:06:37,058 --> 00:06:41,238
The war ended in April 1865,
but in Texas, far from fighting,

116
00:06:41,238 --> 00:06:42,994
slavery carried on for weeks.

117
00:06:43,477 --> 00:06:49,228
On June the 19th, 1865, a Union general
landed at Galveston and read an order.

118
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:51,742
All enslaved people in Texas were free.

119
00:06:51,953 --> 00:06:54,022
That day became Juneteenth.

120
00:06:54,330 --> 00:06:57,483
It started in Texas, and
today it's a national holiday.

121
00:07:00,123 --> 00:07:01,663
Now, remember the dial?

122
00:07:01,936 --> 00:07:04,828
After the war, the federal
government ran Texas for a while.

123
00:07:05,344 --> 00:07:10,086
Formerly enslaved men got the vote, and
most all of them voted for one party,

124
00:07:10,290 --> 00:07:12,674
the party of Lincoln, the Republicans.

125
00:07:12,793 --> 00:07:15,620
For a brief moment, Texas
had Republican government.

126
00:07:16,344 --> 00:07:19,739
In 1870s, Edmund J. Davis became governor.

127
00:07:19,923 --> 00:07:22,427
He was a Texan who had
fought for the Union.

128
00:07:23,136 --> 00:07:26,086
Black Texan were elected to the
legislature for the first time.

129
00:07:26,342 --> 00:07:29,390
Many white Texans saw Davis
government as an occupation.

130
00:07:29,855 --> 00:07:34,115
It had a state police, higher taxes,
and the backing of federal troops.

131
00:07:34,556 --> 00:07:38,324
His supporters saw something else, the
first government in Texas that tried to

132
00:07:38,324 --> 00:07:40,593
protect the rights of Black citizens.

133
00:07:41,107 --> 00:07:42,883
Either way, it did not last.

134
00:07:43,168 --> 00:07:49,550
In the 1873 election, the Democrat Richard
Coke beat Davis by about two to one.

135
00:07:49,714 --> 00:07:51,308
Davis refused to leave at first.

136
00:07:51,784 --> 00:07:55,881
For a few days, Texas had two rivals
government in the same Capitol building.

137
00:07:56,363 --> 00:08:00,065
Then the president, Ulysses
Grant, refused to send troops.

138
00:08:00,239 --> 00:08:01,219
Davis walked out

139
00:08:03,961 --> 00:08:06,953
Here is what most people
get this story backwards.

140
00:08:07,046 --> 00:08:11,248
Democrats who took over in 1874
called themselves the Redeemers.

141
00:08:11,445 --> 00:08:15,854
They wrote a new state constitution
in 1876, still the Constitution of

142
00:08:15,882 --> 00:08:20,002
Texas today, and built a party that
would run Texas for more than 100

143
00:08:20,005 --> 00:08:24,509
years, and that party was openly
committed to white political control

144
00:08:28,029 --> 00:08:33,019
At the start of the 1900s, two events
four months apart changed Texas forever.

145
00:08:33,079 --> 00:08:38,092
On September the 18th, 1900, a
hurricane struck the city of Galveston,

146
00:08:38,337 --> 00:08:39,888
then the biggest port in Texas.

147
00:08:40,168 --> 00:08:42,943
Somewhere between six
and 12,000 people died.

148
00:08:43,034 --> 00:08:47,043
It is still the deadliest natural
disaster in American history.

149
00:08:47,532 --> 00:08:52,627
Then on January the 10th, 1901, near
Beaumont, a well at a hill called

150
00:08:52,632 --> 00:08:56,388
Spindletop blew oil 150 feet into the sky.

151
00:08:56,472 --> 00:09:00,906
The Texas oil boom had begun,
and money started pouring into a

152
00:09:00,907 --> 00:09:06,030
city nearby, one that would grow
to replace Galveston, Houston.

153
00:09:06,925 --> 00:09:07,893
Back to the dial.

154
00:09:07,938 --> 00:09:11,892
It is still locked in blue,
but how do you keep one party

155
00:09:11,892 --> 00:09:13,536
in power for a whole century?

156
00:09:13,827 --> 00:09:15,248
Part of the answer is simple.

157
00:09:15,537 --> 00:09:20,018
For generation of White Texans, voting
Republican meant voting for the party

158
00:09:20,018 --> 00:09:22,097
of the Union Army and Reconstruction.

159
00:09:22,445 --> 00:09:23,747
It just wasn't done.

160
00:09:24,207 --> 00:09:26,902
The other part is about
who was allowed to vote.

161
00:09:27,321 --> 00:09:30,398
In 1901, Texas added a poll tax.

162
00:09:30,748 --> 00:09:32,275
You had to pay to vote.

163
00:09:32,525 --> 00:09:36,251
It hit poor voters of every
race, and especially Black

164
00:09:36,251 --> 00:09:37,588
and Mexican American Texans.

165
00:09:38,901 --> 00:09:40,535
Then became the white primary.

166
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:44,469
The Democratic Party simply barred
Black voter from its primaries,

167
00:09:44,762 --> 00:09:49,082
and since the Democrats always won
in November, the primary was the

168
00:09:49,204 --> 00:09:51,044
only election that had mattered.

169
00:09:51,165 --> 00:09:55,438
A Black dentist from Houston named
Lonnie Smith that took system

170
00:09:55,519 --> 00:09:57,110
all the way to the Supreme Court.

171
00:09:57,356 --> 00:10:02,981
In 1944, in Smith v. Allwright,
the court struck down the white

172
00:10:02,983 --> 00:10:04,565
primary eight votes to one.

173
00:10:04,614 --> 00:10:08,527
The lawyers who argued his case
was a young Thurgood Marshall.

174
00:10:09,064 --> 00:10:12,175
Now, here's the twist that sets
up everything that comes next.

175
00:10:12,386 --> 00:10:16,178
Inside that one giant Democratic
Party, Texans still argued.

176
00:10:16,686 --> 00:10:20,321
There were liberal Democrats and
conservative Democrats, and they fought

177
00:10:20,321 --> 00:10:22,038
each other fiercely in the primaries.

178
00:10:22,066 --> 00:10:26,297
For decades, the conservative wing
usually won, which meant that in

179
00:10:26,303 --> 00:10:29,786
practice, Texas was a conservative
state that voted Democrat.

180
00:10:29,806 --> 00:10:33,477
And that stays one of the
one-party trap I promised you.

181
00:10:33,794 --> 00:10:37,756
When the primary is the only election
that matters, the real fight moves

182
00:10:37,771 --> 00:10:40,414
inside the party, and the party splits.

183
00:10:40,970 --> 00:10:44,534
Keep that in mind, because the moment
those conservative Democrats found a

184
00:10:44,536 --> 00:10:46,659
new home, the dial was going to move.

185
00:10:47,291 --> 00:10:48,792
Two more stages to go.

186
00:10:50,804 --> 00:10:56,370
The first hairline crack came from a
war hero in 1952 and again in 1956.

187
00:10:56,828 --> 00:11:00,405
Texas voted for the Republican
Dwight Eisenhower for president.

188
00:11:00,726 --> 00:11:04,652
Lots of conservative Democrats were
happy to vote Republican at the top of

189
00:11:04,652 --> 00:11:06,998
the ticket and Democrat everywhere else.

190
00:11:07,356 --> 00:11:12,437
Then in 1961, Lyndon Johnson left his
Senate seat to become vice president.

191
00:11:12,566 --> 00:11:14,496
There was a special
election to replace him.

192
00:11:14,686 --> 00:11:16,140
The Democrats were split.

193
00:11:16,441 --> 00:11:20,295
Their candidate was a conservative
oil man, and many liberal Democrats

194
00:11:20,305 --> 00:11:21,779
simply refused to vote for him.

195
00:11:22,404 --> 00:11:23,626
Some of them stayed home.

196
00:11:23,951 --> 00:11:26,707
Some voted Republican
just to send a message.

197
00:11:27,031 --> 00:11:31,986
A young Republican professor named
John Tower won by less than two points.

198
00:11:32,154 --> 00:11:36,617
He became the first Republican elected
statewide in Texas since Reconstruction.

199
00:11:36,884 --> 00:11:42,726
On November 22nd, 1963, President John
F. Kennedy was shot and killed in Dallas.

200
00:11:43,043 --> 00:11:47,379
Lyndon Johnson, a Texan, was sworn
in as president on Air Force One.

201
00:11:47,692 --> 00:11:51,246
The next year, Johnson pushed
through the Civil Rights Act of

202
00:11:51,284 --> 00:11:56,590
1964, outlawing segregation, then
the Voting Rights Act of 1965.

203
00:11:57,004 --> 00:12:00,340
There is a famous line that
Johnson said the Democrats had just

204
00:12:00,781 --> 00:12:02,376
lost the South for a generation.

205
00:12:02,645 --> 00:12:06,983
Historians argue about the whether
he really said it and exactly

206
00:12:06,987 --> 00:12:09,105
how, but the directions was clear.

207
00:12:09,438 --> 00:12:13,960
The National Democratic Party was
now the party of civil rights, and

208
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:17,168
many conservative white Southerners
began to look for the door

209
00:12:21,044 --> 00:12:23,734
It took a long time, too
long for anyone in a hurry.

210
00:12:23,927 --> 00:12:28,743
In 1978, an oilman named Bill Clements
became the first Republican governor

211
00:12:28,785 --> 00:12:30,869
of Texas in more than 100 years.

212
00:12:30,886 --> 00:12:33,023
He won by less than one percentage point.

213
00:12:33,182 --> 00:12:37,440
In 1980, Ronald Reagan carried
Texas, so did every Republican

214
00:12:37,442 --> 00:12:39,034
presidential candidate after him.

215
00:12:39,430 --> 00:12:42,378
And conservative Democrats
started switching parties.

216
00:12:42,774 --> 00:12:45,954
A congressman named Phil
Gramm switched in 1983.

217
00:12:46,076 --> 00:12:49,487
A young legislator named
Rick Perry switched in 1989.

218
00:12:49,780 --> 00:12:55,499
In 1994, George W. Bush beat the popular
Democratic Governor Ann Richards.

219
00:12:55,633 --> 00:13:00,910
That same year, Democrats won their last
statewide race in Texas for 32 years.

220
00:13:01,311 --> 00:13:06,075
In 2002, Republicans won the Texas House
for the first time since Reconstruction.

221
00:13:06,094 --> 00:13:08,191
The aisle was finally completely red.

222
00:13:08,487 --> 00:13:10,223
So why did Texas turn red?

223
00:13:10,554 --> 00:13:12,116
It depends on who you ask.

224
00:13:12,249 --> 00:13:16,096
Republicans and many conservatives
say Texas didn't really change,

225
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:18,132
the national Democratic Party did.

226
00:13:18,388 --> 00:13:23,019
It moved left on Texas, guns,
abortion, and the role of government,

227
00:13:23,030 --> 00:13:25,202
while Texans stayed where they were.

228
00:13:25,879 --> 00:13:29,855
Add oil money, booming suburbs,
and millions of newcomers from

229
00:13:29,903 --> 00:13:33,894
other state who wanted low taxes
and yet get Republican Texas.

230
00:13:34,358 --> 00:13:38,897
Critics and many historians of the
South say race was at the heart of it.

231
00:13:39,132 --> 00:13:42,672
The switch picked up the speed
right after the civil rights laws,

232
00:13:42,995 --> 00:13:46,401
and since then, they argue the
district maps and voting rules have

233
00:13:46,494 --> 00:13:48,197
helped lock in Republican power.

234
00:13:48,496 --> 00:13:52,920
Most historians would say it was
some of both, and it happened slowly,

235
00:13:53,017 --> 00:13:55,447
over 30 years, not in one night

236
00:13:59,385 --> 00:14:03,289
